92186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d443cf2-e450-59f7-912e-8007eb24ac35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162625 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are orthogonal. Finally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1061 30532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a486be1-0f5d-50ea-a245-3aedf2c597b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1061 30594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34cd3b8c-2df0-5886-a6f1-17125ede8748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162871 #edb/ref 1061 30595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162871 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9df74bd-9e6b-5113-a23f-86c30ccc850b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162871 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1061 30597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9795272-744c-5dd3-afc9-73a980ee4bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162872 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1061 31356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "63db5c7a-ccd4-5948-af8b-421d4a60d2da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a real matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nThe procedure for finding an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ is similar to diagonalizing a regular (diagonalizable) $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nAs an example, consider the matrix \n![](images/ac/acba5373e2f2470930adec855df9958508d9f41aba5d3fc6988b0001c07a9dfd.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. So let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\qquad \\lambda_2=-6, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-9.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** We need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/cd/cddf44571b3aba21dca82cc12664733469c17f30ed3a9a9cfc62acb12230caef.svg)\nNotice that the eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal. \nNow, an orthogonal diagonalization requires each eigenvector to be normalized. So, dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the following unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/e4/e4bfc5b51cea64bd5cb8d340fbeab1a126aaa3f48ae42b991c2b5af2a25480b3.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/4b/4b4a8c98b7c4688fab231510b6b1d615f47bed070310d39f3e812cd9fb811395.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, we automatically get orthogonal eigenvectors because $A$ has three *distinct* eigenvalues. However, in the case of repeated eigenvalues, the corresponding eigenvectors might not be orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1065 4881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ff932ad-00a4-58cd-952f-55c21e02ef43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163223 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1065 5013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ad655c9-a00e-5174-a98f-6ae8ab07843d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163249 #edb/ref 1067 "Symmetric Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355074 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355074 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355074 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f1ffe36-0882-5870-a270-de42a9825f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355082 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355082 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355082 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30058854-8d3f-57d9-be58-54e0a4a41e2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355303 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355303 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355303 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47e9181e-3767-5221-bf98-12ae5e9c516c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355528 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355528 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/re