-9de6-7f832f534bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363202 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "312d113a-1309-533b-bf4f-3d3a3da1e08c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394963 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bac7e35-18fd-5d08-a4e1-72c5d7aa78ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394963 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394964 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2d3ea6d-180a-530e-a554-9eaf5810635a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394965 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394965 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394965 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "515e7963-43a8-5d4c-b890-b12012ce6c51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394966 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28470fa3-1d40-50b2-a80d-1ee99e65ae81" #edb/ref 17592186045417