rix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=7.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1045 "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186505873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0eb4fea8